# Livewire actions inside teleported modals

`flux:modal` (and any UI that **teleports** markup) moves DOM **outside** the Livewire `wire:id` root. Alpine `$wire` and `wire:click` then run on the **wrong** component — usually the **page that owns the modal**, not a nested child rendered inside it.

Symptom: `Livewire\Exceptions\MethodNotFoundException` — `Unable to call component method. Public method [x] not found on component`.

This is not specific to one feature. It applies wherever a nested Livewire child (or Flux control) sits inside a teleported modal.

## Do not

- Put `wire:click="childMethod"` on `flux:button` (especially **icon-only / self-closing**) inside a teleported modal when `childMethod` lives on a **nested** Livewire component.
- Assume Flux forwards `wire:click`, `x-on:click`, and `data-*` to the real `<button>`. Often they stay on a wrapper; the click hits the parent `$wire`.
- Treat `Livewire::test(Child::class)->call('childMethod')` as proof the **button** works. That call never goes through the parent or the modal.
- Treat Pest Browser `waitForText` / `assertDontSee` as enough. Livewire `MethodNotFound` is a **server** error overlay, not a JavaScript exception.

`wire:submit` on a form that Livewire compiled on the child can still work. **Click handlers** on Flux/Alpine inside the teleported tree are the failure mode.

## Do

1. Put the public action on the Livewire component that **owns the modal** (`wire:model` / `wire:model.self` on that `flux:modal`).
2. Invoke it the same way as other modal actions in that page: a **native** `<button type="button">` (not `flux:button`) with explicit `Livewire.find(...)`.
3. Pass the owner component id into nested views (`:parent-component-id="$this->getId()"` or equivalent). Put `data-*` on the **same** node that has `x-on:click`.
4. After the owner mutates nested state, `$this->dispatch('...')` and let the child `#[On('...')]` refresh computed lists.
5. Optionally keep the same method on the child for isolated tests; the **UI path** must call the owner.

```blade
{{-- Owner view: capture id once, pass into nested child --}}
@php($ownerComponentId = $this->getId())

<livewire:domain.nested-panel
    :parent-component-id="$ownerComponentId"
    :key="'nested-'.$record->id"
/>
```

```blade
{{-- Nested (or owner) markup inside flux:modal --}}
<button
    type="button"
    data-test="record-delete-{{ $record->id }}"
    data-index-component-id="{{ $parentComponentId }}"
    data-record-id="{{ $record->id }}"
    x-on:click.stop="Livewire.find($event.currentTarget.dataset.indexComponentId).deleteRecord(Number($event.currentTarget.dataset.recordId))"
>
    {{ __('Delete') }}
</button>
```

```php
// On the modal-owning component
public function deleteRecord(int $recordId): void
{
    // authorize + delete scoped to the open record
    $this->dispatch('nested-panel-refresh');
}
```

## Tests (mandatory for this pattern)

Feature — go through the **owner**, and assert wiring in the rendered HTML:

```php
Livewire::test(OwnerPage::class)
    ->call('openDetailModal', $record->id)
    ->call('deleteRecord', $item->id)
    ->assertSeeHtml('Livewire.find($event.currentTarget.dataset.indexComponentId)')
    ->assertDontSeeHtml('wire:click="deleteRecord');
```

Browser — click the real control on the page that contains the modal:

- `assertNoJavaScriptErrors()`
- `assertDontSee('Unable to call component method')`
- then assert persistence / UI outcome (`waitForText`, missing row, etc.)

Isolated `Livewire::test(Child::class)->call(...)` is extra coverage only. It does **not** replace the owner + browser tests above.
